        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 136, nm: 320 }, stage1: { hp: 185, nm: 400 }, stage2: { hp: 205, nm: 430 }, stage3: { hp: 245, nm: 480 } },
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/audi/A4/B8/b8.glb", model3DScale: 0.25, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: -1, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.14 , y: -0.02, z: 0.49 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["glass.001"], taillights: ["MaterialReflector"], drllight: ["MaterialLowAndHighBeam"] }, author: "Audi A4 B8 (https://skfb.ly/pEYpx) by Ruleta Dev is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 7, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 500, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 1.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1 },
        drivingExperience: "The 136hp CAGB ('TDIe') provides a refined and torquey driving experience optimized entirely for fuel economy. Thanks to the Common Rail injection system, it is remarkably smooth. The 320 Nm of torque provides decent punch in city traffic, but the longer gear ratios of the TDIe versions mean it can feel slightly sluggish when accelerating at highway speeds without downshifting. It is an exceptional long-distance cruiser that begs to be mapped to unleash its true potential.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The CAGB 2.0 TDI EA189 is an incredibly robust engine block that suffers from two major accessory flaws. The CP4 high-pressure fuel pump is a known failure point that can total the fuel system, and the oil pump hex shaft will round off and destroy the engine if not upgraded. If you proactively install a hardened hex shaft and a CP4 bypass kit, this engine will easily reach 400,000 km. Flash it to Stage 1 to remove the software restrictions, and you have a phenomenal, highly reliable daily driver.",
